---
import TopicPage from '../../../../components/TopicPage.astro';
import Widget from '../../../../components/Widget.astro';

const projCfg = {
  preset: 'projectile',
  params: [
    { name: 'u', label: 'launch speed u', min: 5, max: 40, step: 1, value: 20, unit: 'm/s' },
    { name: 'theta', label: 'launch angle θ', min: 10, max: 80, step: 1, value: 45, unit: '°' },
    { name: 'g', label: 'gravity g', min: 1.6, max: 25, step: 0.1, value: 9.8, unit: 'm/s²' },
  ],
};

const prDialsMatch = {
  "prompt": "A 20 m/s launch at 40° has been split. Match each piece to its role:",
  "pairs": [
    {
      "left": "u·cosθ = 15.3 m/s",
      "right": "the horizontal dial — gravity never touches it"
    },
    {
      "left": "u·sinθ = 12.9 m/s",
      "right": "the vertical dial — gravity runs it down 9.8 m/s every second"
    },
    {
      "left": "t, the flight time",
      "right": "the glue between the two problems"
    },
    {
      "left": "θ, the launch angle",
      "right": "the choice that splits one speed into two independent dials"
    }
  ],
  "why": "Two independent dials and one shared clock: 'solve the projectile' means solving the vertical problem for times and heights, the horizontal for distances."
};

const prApexPredict = {
  "q": "A projectile launched at 30° reaches the top of its flight. Which is true at that instant?",
  "choices": [
    "The projectile stops completely for an instant",
    "It still moves at its full horizontal speed — 17.3 m/s for a 20 m/s launch",
    "Its horizontal speed has halved since launch"
  ],
  "answer": 1,
  "why": "It stops RISING — the vertical component is zero for an instant — but nothing removes the horizontal component, and the downward acceleration never pauses."
};

const prTowerEstimate = {
  "prompt": "A ball fired horizontally at 300 m/s from a 45 m tower lands after 3.03 s — same as the dropped ball. Roughly how far away does it land?",
  "unit": "m",
  "answer": 909,
  "factor": 1.5,
  "why": "Range = 300 × 3.03 ≈ 909 m — farther, but not later: both balls strike the ground simultaneously.",
  "context": "Drop time depends only on the vertical problem: t = √(2h/g). No mass appears anywhere."
};

const prPathBuckets = {
  "prompt": "Match each scenario to the path it traces:",
  "buckets": [
    "Parabola",
    "Circle",
    "Straight line"
  ],
  "items": [
    {
      "label": "a horizontal throw, with gravity pulling down throughout",
      "bucket": 0
    },
    {
      "label": "electrons steered by an electric force, as in old cathode-ray tubes",
      "bucket": 0
    },
    {
      "label": "a magnetic force acting sideways on a moving charge",
      "bucket": 1
    },
    {
      "label": "deep space, after the engines cut and no force remains",
      "bucket": 2
    },
    {
      "label": "a ball thrown off a 45 m tower",
      "bucket": 0
    }
  ],
  "why": "A vertical acceleration layered on steady horizontal motion is a quadratic in x — a parabola. A sideways force bends the path into a circle; no force leaves a straight line."
};

const prAngleJudge = {
  "statements": [
    {
      "s": "45° maximises range on level ground with no air.",
      "truth": true,
      "why": "sin2θ peaks at 2θ = 90° — the textbook rule, for the textbook model."
    },
    {
      "s": "A 30° drive and a 60° lob land together.",
      "truth": true,
      "why": "Complementary angles share range — the lob just takes longer, useful for clearing obstacles."
    },
    {
      "s": "45° is the best angle for a shot released two metres above the ground.",
      "truth": false,
      "why": "With a launch-height difference (or drag) the optimum drops below 45° — the rule is the ideal model's, not your arm's."
    },
    {
      "s": "A 20° launch reaches about 68% of maximum range.",
      "truth": true,
      "why": "The sensitivity is asymmetric: steep angles near 45° lose little, shallow angles lose it quickly."
    }
  ]
};

const prAirCloze = {
  "text": "Drag grows with speed squared and steals horizontal speed continuously: a cricket ball thrown flat out flies farthest at roughly %%0%%°, a raindrop's descent is capped at about %%1%% m/s, and a spread-eagled skydiver's at about %%2%% m/s.",
  "answers": [
    [
      "35",
      "thirty-five"
    ],
    [
      "9",
      "nine"
    ],
    [
      "55",
      "fifty-five"
    ]
  ],
  "hint": "One number per blank — two from the falling caps, one from the cricket ball's best angle."
};
---
<TopicPage slug="subjects/physics/intermediate/projectile-and-2d-motion">
  <h2>Where you are, and what you will learn</h2>
  <p>
    This is an intermediate lesson in Physics, and it is where the foundational lessons start
    paying out. From Motion in One Dimension you need the constant-acceleration equations — the
    vertical problem here is exactly that chapter's free fall. From Newton's Three Laws you
    need inertia: the insight that a horizontal velocity persists unless a force removes it.
    And Energy and Its Conservation returns in the deeper sections as a check on every speed
    this page computes. One new idea does all the work: split the motion into two independent
    problems and let time glue them back together.
  </p>
  <p>By the end you will be able to:</p>
  <ul>
    <li>resolve any launch velocity into horizontal and vertical components, and explain why the two axes never interfere;</li>
    <li>run the five-number audit — components, time up, flight time, peak height, range — for any ideal projectile;</li>
    <li>explain why the path is a parabola and why landing speed equals launch speed on level ground;</li>
    <li>use the range formula R = u²·sin2θ/g to account for the 45° rule and complementary angles;</li>
    <li>predict how drag and non-level launches shift real trajectories below the ideal model.</li>
  </ul>

  <h2>The idea</h2>
  <p>
    The trick that unlocks all two-dimensional motion: <strong>horizontal and vertical motion
    are independent</strong>. A bullet fired horizontally and a bullet dropped from the same
    height hit the ground at the same instant — gravity only edits the vertical account, and
    nothing reaches over to touch the horizontal one. A projectile is therefore two easy
    problems wearing one costume: constant velocity sideways, constant acceleration downward.
    Galileo saw this in 1638, and it is still the single most transferable idea in mechanics:
    once you can split any motion into components and solve each separately, you can handle
    cannonballs, basketballs, water jets and — with a tweak of the same logic — satellites.
  </p>

  <Widget no="1" caption="launch it — set gravity to the Moon's 1.6 and watch the same throw become a monster" widget="sliderLab" config={projCfg} />
  <p>
    <strong>What to notice:</strong> three dials — launch speed u, launch angle θ, and gravity
    g. First leave u and θ alone and drag g from Earth's 9.8 down to the Moon's 1.6: the very
    same throw becomes a 250-metre punt, because range divides by g. Restore Earth and sweep θ:
    the range peaks at 45°, and the twin angles 30° and 60° land together by different routes.
    Then push u upward and watch range grow faster than the speed — it scales with u², the
    same squaring that made kinetic energy outrun speed in the energy lesson.
  </p>

  <p>
    Three things to discover with the sliders. <strong>Range is maximised at 45°</strong> on
    level ground (any higher buys height it can't spend, any lower wastes speed on a flat
    trajectory). <strong>Complementary angles</strong> (30° and 60°) give the same range by
    different routes — a high slow lob and a low fast drive. And lowering g stretches
    everything: the Moon's gravity turns a 20 m/s throw into a 250-metre punt.
  </p>

  <h2>Vectors: splitting motion into components</h2>
  <p>
    First the bookkeeping. Some quantities are scalars (mass, time, energy — a number and a
    unit); others are <strong>vectors</strong> (displacement, velocity, acceleration, force —
    a number <em>and</em> a direction). Vectors add tip-to-tail, but the productive move is the
    reverse: resolve any vector into horizontal and vertical components with trigonometry. A
    launch at speed u and angle θ has components u·cosθ (horizontal) and u·sinθ (vertical).
    Worked example: 20 m/s at 40° gives 20 × cos 40° = 15.3 m/s horizontally and
    20 × sin 40° = 12.9 m/s vertically. Notice the deep point hiding here: those two numbers
    are <em>independent dials</em>. Gravity will run one dial down at 9.8 m/s every second and
    never know the other exists. From now on, "solve the projectile" means "solve the vertical
    problem for times and heights, the horizontal problem for distances, and let t glue them
    together".
  </p>
  <Widget no="2" caption="pair each quantity with its role in the split" widget="match" config={prDialsMatch} />
  <p><em>Common misconception:</em> the horizontal component slows down as the projectile climbs and speeds up again as it falls. Only drag does that. In the model the horizontal component never changes; climbing and falling are entirely vertical events, which is precisely why the components can be solved separately.</p>
  <Widget no="3" caption="bet first — the top of the flight" widget="predict" config={prApexPredict} />

  <h2>The independence principle, demonstrated</h2>
  <p>
    The classic proof needs no algebra. Fire a ball horizontally at 300 m/s from a 45 m tower
    and drop an identical ball at the same instant. The drop time depends only on the vertical
    problem: t = √(2h/g) = √(2 × 45/9.8) = <strong>3.03 s</strong> — same for both. The fired
    ball meanwhile lands 300 × 3.03 ≈ <strong>909 m</strong> away. It travelled farther, but
    not <em>later</em>: both strike the ground simultaneously (a real demonstration, and the
    point of Galileo's inclined-plane slow-motion versions). No mass appears anywhere in these
    equations: heavy or light, the fall is identical, because gravitational force grows with
    mass exactly in step with inertia's reluctance to accelerate. The medieval intuition —
    heavier falls faster — survives only where air resistance matters, which is a property of
    the <em>fluid</em>, not the law of falling.
  </p>
  <Widget no="4" caption="call the magnitude — the tower demonstration's range" widget="estimate" config={prTowerEstimate} />
  <p><em>Common misconception:</em> "something must keep pushing the projectile along
  horizontally." Nothing does. After launch, the only force is gravity (plus drag); the
  horizontal velocity persists because nothing <em>removes</em> it — inertia, discovered
  precisely by asking why arrows keep flying. The horizontal component is a number, not a
  fuel supply. A second resident error: "at the top of the flight the projectile stops." It
  stops <em>rising</em> — the vertical component is zero for an instant — but the horizontal
  component is untouched, and the acceleration downward never pauses. A 30° launch at the
  apex is still moving at its full 17.3 m/s horizontally.</p>
  <p>
    <strong>So far:</strong> the tower demonstration turns independence from a slogan into a
    measurement — the fired ball and the dropped one land together. What remains is to run the
    method at full power on a single kick, top to bottom.
  </p>

  <h2>The full worked example</h2>
  <p>
    Take a football kicked at 20 m/s at 30°, and run the whole audit. Components: horizontal
    20 cos 30° = 17.3 m/s; vertical 20 sin 30° = 10 m/s. <strong>Time up:</strong> vertical
    speed falls by 9.8 each second, so it reaches zero (the top) after 10/9.8 = 1.02 s.
    <strong>Time of flight:</strong> what goes up comes down to the same level, so double it:
    2.04 s. <strong>Maximum height:</strong> y = u·sinθ·t − ½gt² at the top, or
    (10)²/(2 × 9.8) = <strong>5.1 m</strong>. <strong>Range:</strong> horizontal speed × total
    time = 17.3 × 2.04 = <strong>35.3 m</strong> — exactly what the range formula
    R = u²·sin2θ/g gives (400 × sin 60°/9.8). Every question a coach, an artillery officer or
    a games programmer asks is one of these five numbers, and the method never changes:
    vertical problem first, horizontal arithmetic second. The horizontal-launch case is the
    same audit with one dial preset to zero: a ball rolled off a 45 m cliff at 20 m/s takes
    √(2 × 45/9.8) = 3.03 s to fall and lands 20 × 3.03 ≈ 61 m out — three numbers instead of
    five, identical logic.
  </p>
  <p>
    <strong>So far:</strong> the audit produced five numbers from two dials, and the method —
    vertical problem first, horizontal arithmetic second — never changes. The next question is
    about shape rather than numbers: what curve has the projectile been tracing all along?
  </p>

  <h2>Why the path is a parabola</h2>
  <p>
    Eliminate time between the two equations (from x = u·cosθ·t, write t = x/(u·cosθ) and
    substitute into the vertical equation) and you get y = x·tanθ − g·x²/(2u²cos²θ) — a
    quadratic in x: a <strong>parabola</strong>. The path is curved because the vertical
    position picks up a t² term while the horizontal does not; the asymmetry between the axes
    is what curvature <em>is</em>. The same elimination, with gravity replaced by electric
    force, traces the parabolas of electrons in old cathode-ray tubes; with a magnetic force
    (which acts sideways), the path becomes a circle instead — the shape of the path tells you
    the geometry of the force.
  </p>
  <Widget no="5" caption="file the paths — the shape of the path is the geometry of the force" widget="sort-buckets" config={prPathBuckets} />
  <p><em>Common misconception:</em> the path bends because the projectile is slowing down. Gravity bends the velocity's <em>direction</em>, not (on level ground, without drag) its size: the landing speed equals the launch speed. A projectile can be curving the whole way yet never lose a joule of speed.</p>

  <h2>Angles, and the 45° rule</h2>
  <p>
    The range formula R = u²·sin2θ/g explains both slider discoveries. Since sin2θ peaks where
    2θ = 90°, <strong>θ = 45° maximises range</strong> — but note the sensitivity is
    asymmetric: sin2° near 90° falls slowly, so steep angles near 45° lose little range, while
    shallow angles lose it quickly (a 20° launch only reaches 68% of maximum). Complementary
    angles share range because sin(2(90° − θ)) = sin(180° − 2θ) = sin 2θ: the 30° drive and
    60° lob land together, the lob taking longer (useful when clearing an obstacle). For
    different launch and landing heights the optimum shifts below 45° — which is one reason
    the real world never quite obeys the textbook.
  </p>
  <Widget no="6" caption="true or false — the 45° rule and its fine print" widget="judge" config={prAngleJudge} />
  <p><em>Common misconception:</em> 45° is the best angle for every throw. The derivation assumes level ground and no air; release the shot from two metres above the ground, or add drag, and the optimum drops below 45°. The rule is a property of the ideal model, not of your arm.</p>
  <p>
    <strong>Real air changes the numbers.</strong> Drag grows with speed squared and steals
    horizontal speed continuously, so real projectiles fall short of the parabola and their
    optimal launch angles drop: a cricket ball thrown flat out flies farthest at roughly 35°,
    a javelin (which also gains lift from its shape) similar, and a golf drive's effective
    optimum trades launch angle against spin's lift and drag in ways professional ballistics
    models in detail. Terminal speed caps any falling object's descent — a raindrop lands at
    about 9 m/s, a skydiver spread-eagled at about 55 m/s — while the vacuum parabola has no
    such ceiling. Treat the parabola as the first model, the one whose errors tell you how
    strong the air is.
  </p>
  <Widget no="7" caption="fill the blanks — what real air does to the parabola" widget="cloze" config={prAirCloze} />

  <h2>Check yourself</h2>
  <p>Try these before moving on. Feedback follows each answer.</p>
  <ol>
    <li>At the very top of its flight, what is a projectile's vertical velocity, its horizontal velocity, and its acceleration?</li>
    <li>A stone is thrown horizontally at 15 m/s from a 20 m cliff. How long is it in the air, and how far from the base does it land?</li>
    <li>Why does 45° maximise range on level ground, and why does releasing a shot from 2 m above the ground spoil that rule?</li>
  </ol>
  <p>
    <strong>Feedback:</strong> (1) Vertical velocity: zero — it has finished rising and not yet
    begun falling. Horizontal velocity: still its full launch value, u·cosθ, untouched by
    gravity. Acceleration: 9.8 m/s² downward, unpaused — if it were zero at the top the ball
    would hover there. (2) The vertical problem sets the clock: t = √(2h/g) =
    √(2 × 20/9.8) ≈ 2.02 s. The horizontal problem then just multiplies: 15 × 2.02 ≈ 30 m.
    Notice the thrown stone and a dropped one leave the cliff top and hit the ground together.
    (3) The range formula R = u²·sin2θ/g peaks where 2θ = 90°, so θ = 45° — but only when
    launch and landing heights match. Raise the release point and the extra fall time rewards
    a flatter, faster trajectory, so the optimum slides below 45°; add drag and it slides
    further, since less time aloft means less horizontal speed stolen. The rule belongs to the
    ideal model, not to your arm.
  </p>

  <h2>Key takeaways</h2>
  <ul>
    <li>Horizontal and vertical motion are independent: gravity edits only the vertical account, and time t glues the two problems back together.</li>
    <li>A launch at speed u and angle θ splits into u·cosθ (constant all flight) and u·sinθ (losing 9.8 m/s of upward speed every second).</li>
    <li>The five-number audit — components, time up, flight time, maximum height, range — solves every ideal projectile; vertical problem first.</li>
    <li>The path is a parabola because the vertical position gains a t² term the horizontal never acquires.</li>
    <li>Range R = u²·sin2θ/g: maximal at 45° on level ground; complementary angles (30°, 60°) land together.</li>
    <li>Drag steals horizontal speed continuously, so real projectiles fall short of the parabola and fly farthest below 45°.</li>
    <li>Newton's escalation: a projectile fast enough that the ground curves away as fast as it falls is in orbit.</li>
  </ul>

  <h2>Where this goes next</h2>
  <p>
    The sibling lesson Circular Motion &amp; Gravitation picks up the thread this page ends on:
    Newton's projectile that never lands is an orbit, and the same component logic extends to
    circular paths. Momentum &amp; Collisions then takes over at the landing — what the
    projectile's mass times velocity does when the flight finally stops.
  </p>

  <h2>Going deeper</h2>
  <p>
    <strong>Sign conventions: where the marks are lost.</strong> The equations are simple; the
    errors are bookkeeping. Choose an axis direction — say up positive — and obey it everywhere:
    gravity is then a = −9.8 m/s² <em>always</em>, including on the way down and at the top. A
    downward launch speed enters as a negative u·sinθ. The trap cases: forgetting that the
    displacement from launch to landing on a lower surface is negative; writing ½gt² with the
    wrong sign; and the classic "at the highest point the velocity and acceleration are both
    zero". Check the last one against the physics: at the apex the vertical velocity is
    momentarily zero, but the ball is still in free fall — acceleration is 9.8 m/s² downward
    through every instant of the flight. If it were zero at the top, the ball would hover. A
    useful audit: the v² = u² − 2gy relation (no time in it) gives the speed at any height in
    one step, and it must return the same speed coming down as going up through each height.
  </p>
  <p>
    <strong>Energy bookkeeping along the path.</strong> Ignoring drag, mechanical energy is
    conserved: kinetic + gravitational potential stays constant. The speed is greatest at
    launch and landing, and <em>minimum at the top</em> — but not zero: at the apex the
    vertical component has gone, yet the horizontal component remains, so v_top = u·cosθ. A
    20 m/s, 30° launch slows from 20 m/s to 17.3 m/s at its 5.1 m peak — the kinetic energy
    lost (½m(20² − 17.3²) = 51m joules) is exactly the potential energy gained (m·9.8·5.1 =
    50m, rounding). This is also why the landing speed equals the launch speed at equal
    heights: the energy ledger must balance.
  </p>
  <p>
    <strong>Relative velocity: motion is a two-way street.</strong> All velocities are
    measured relative to some frame, and components make frame-switching mechanical. A rower
    who can do 3 m/s in still water, crossing a river flowing 2 m/s: aim straight across and
    drift 2 m downstream per second of crossing — or aim upstream at the angle that cancels
    the flow (sin θ = 2/3, θ ≈ 42°) and land directly opposite, taking longer. An aircraft
    with airspeed 250 m/s in a 50 m/s headwind has ground speed 200 m/s; add a crosswind and
    the pilot must aim crabwise — the same vector arithmetic, at scale. One consistency check
    ties this page together: in the <em>air's</em> frame, a projectile's motion is the clean
    parabola; the wind simply adds a uniform drift to the whole picture, because adding a
    constant velocity bends no path.
  </p>
  <p>
    <strong>Before Galileo, the guesswork.</strong> Artillery officers in the 1500s assumed the
    path was a straight line, then a circular arc, then a straight drop — Niccolò Tartaglia's
    <em>La Nova Scientia</em> (1537) built firing tables on that three-piece guess and was
    still the best available. Galileo's insight that the parabola emerges from <em>two uniform
    motions compounded</em> replaced the guess with a derivation — one of the first times a
    mathematical model predicted a natural trajectory rather than tabulating it. The artillery
    that adopted it paid better than the artillery that didn't; war, as ever, funded the
    physics.
  </p>
  <p>
    <strong>The projectile that never lands.</strong> Newton's own escalation of this chapter:
    fire the cannonball faster and faster from an idealised mountain. Range grows — until the
    ball's curvature matches Earth's, and the ground curves away exactly as fast as the ball
    falls. The ball is now in <strong>orbit</strong>: perpetually falling, perpetually missing.
    The required speed is √(gR) = √(9.8 × 6.37 × 10⁶) ≈ 7.9 km/s. Every satellite, and every
    astronaut, is a projectile solving the equations on this page with a very large launch
    speed — the circular-motion chapter picks the thread up from there.
  </p>
  <p>
    <strong>How g varies — and what the slider is really for.</strong> Set the gravity slider
    low and everything stretches: on the Moon (g = 1.6 m/s²) the 20 m/s, 45° throw flies
    250 m and stays aloft 25 s; on Mars (3.7) it carries 108 m; in Jupiter's cloud tops (25)
    a meagre 16 m. Beyond the solar system, the same equations run in reverse: astronomers
    time how long ejected material hangs above a neutron star's surface to weigh the star.
    The slider's lesson generalises: the <em>shape</em> of motion is set by the model; the
    <em>numbers</em> are set by the environment. Physics keeps the two separate — that
    separation is why one set of equations suffices for the universe.
  </p>
  <p class="sources"><strong>Sources:</strong> Galileo, Two New Sciences (1638); OpenStax, College Physics (ch. 3, Two-Dimensional Kinematics); NASA planetary fact sheets (surface gravities); standard ballistics treatments of drag.</p>
</TopicPage>
